1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,883
It's 1:23 in the morning on April twenty-sixth, 1986.

2
00:00:03,883 --> 00:00:07,253
A routine safety test is underway at a nuclear

3
00:00:07,253 --> 00:00:10,843
power plant in northern Ukraine, then part of the

4
00:00:10,843 --> 00:00:15,312
Soviet Union. Seconds later, Reactor 4 explodes. What follows

5
00:00:15,312 --> 00:00:18,829
is the worst nuclear accident in history. If you

6
00:00:18,829 --> 00:00:22,492
had been standing near that reactor, you would not

7
00:00:22,492 --> 00:00:26,449
have survived. Today we'll see why, and what Chernobyl

8
00:00:26,449 --> 00:00:27,328
is like now.

9
00:00:27,878 --> 00:00:31,421
Welcome to Earth Decoded. If you love the most

10
00:00:31,421 --> 00:00:35,810
extreme places on our planet, tap subscribe. Today, we're

11
00:00:35,810 --> 00:00:38,737
entering the Chernobyl Exclusion Zone.

12
00:00:39,287 --> 00:00:43,261
The Chernobyl Nuclear Power Plant sits about one hundred

13
00:00:43,261 --> 00:00:46,809
kilometers north of Kyiv, close to the border with

14
00:00:46,809 --> 00:00:50,002
Belarus. Next to it was Pripyat, a young city

15
00:00:50,002 --> 00:00:53,834
built for the plant's workers and their families, home

16
00:00:53,834 --> 00:00:56,460
to around forty-nine thousand people.

17
00:00:57,010 --> 00:01:00,999
That night, operators were testing how the reactor would

18
00:01:00,999 --> 00:01:04,489
behave during a power failure. A combination of a

19
00:01:04,489 --> 00:01:08,335
flawed reactor design and broken safety rules made the

20
00:01:08,335 --> 00:01:10,258
power surge out of control.

21
00:01:10,808 --> 00:01:14,750
Steam explosions blew the reactor apart and threw the

22
00:01:14,750 --> 00:01:18,841
heavy concrete lid off. The graphite inside caught fire

23
00:01:18,841 --> 00:01:23,229
and burned for about ten days, sending radioactive material

24
00:01:23,229 --> 00:01:24,568
high into the sky.

25
00:01:25,118 --> 00:01:30,263
Winds carried that radioactive cloud across Belarus, Russia, Ukraine

26
00:01:30,263 --> 00:01:33,895
and much of Europe. It was first detected abroad

27
00:01:33,895 --> 00:01:37,073
when alarms went off at a nuclear plant in

28
00:01:37,073 --> 00:01:40,478
Sweden, more than a thousand kilometers away.

29
00:01:41,028 --> 00:01:44,757
So what would radiation have done to you? Radiation

30
00:01:44,757 --> 00:01:47,902
is invisible. You can't see it, smell it or

31
00:01:47,902 --> 00:01:51,046
feel it. But at high doses, it destroys the

32
00:01:51,046 --> 00:01:54,410
cells in your body faster than they can repair

33
00:01:54,410 --> 00:01:55,215
themselves.

34
00:01:55,765 --> 00:01:59,827
Near the burning reactor that night, radiation levels were

35
00:01:59,827 --> 00:02:02,908
so high that a person could receive a deadly

36
00:02:02,908 --> 00:02:08,021
dose within minutes. Firefighters arrived without proper protection, many

37
00:02:08,021 --> 00:02:10,122
of them unaware of the danger.

38
00:02:10,672 --> 00:02:15,354
Acute radiation sickness starts with nausea and vomiting, then

39
00:02:15,354 --> 00:02:18,601
burns on the skin, hair loss, and damage to

40
00:02:18,601 --> 00:02:21,773
the bone marrow, so the body can no longer

41
00:02:21,773 --> 00:02:22,981
fight infection.

42
00:02:23,531 --> 00:02:27,175
Two workers died in the explosion. In the following

43
00:02:27,175 --> 00:02:32,176
months, twenty-eight more, mostly firefighters and plant workers, died

44
00:02:32,176 --> 00:02:34,177
of acute radiation sickness.

45
00:02:34,727 --> 00:02:38,855
The long-term toll is still debated. Thousands of children

46
00:02:38,855 --> 00:02:43,340
who drank contaminated milk later developed thyroid cancer, and

47
00:02:43,340 --> 00:02:47,682
health agencies estimate thousands more deaths over the years

48
00:02:47,682 --> 00:02:49,319
among the most exposed.

49
00:02:49,869 --> 00:02:53,323
Quick question. Would you ever visit Chernobyl? Tell me

50
00:02:53,323 --> 00:02:54,327
in the comments.

51
00:02:54,877 --> 00:02:59,829
Pripyat wasn't evacuated until the next afternoon, about thirty-six

52
00:02:59,829 --> 00:03:03,599
hours after the explosion. People were told to pack

53
00:03:03,599 --> 00:03:06,333
for three days. They never came back.

54
00:03:06,883 --> 00:03:10,748
In total, more than three hundred thousand people were

55
00:03:10,748 --> 00:03:14,827
eventually moved from their homes in Ukraine and Belarus.

56
00:03:14,827 --> 00:03:18,477
Around the plant, a zone of about thirty kilometers

57
00:03:18,477 --> 00:03:21,411
was closed: the Chernobyl Exclusion Zone.

58
00:03:21,961 --> 00:03:25,565
Today, Pripyat is frozen in time. A Ferris wheel

59
00:03:25,565 --> 00:03:29,843
that was never officially used, empty schools, a swimming

60
00:03:29,843 --> 00:03:33,972
pool, apartment blocks slowly being swallowed by trees.

61
00:03:34,522 --> 00:03:38,014
Near the plant, a patch of pine forest absorbed

62
00:03:38,014 --> 00:03:41,581
so much radiation that the trees died and turned

63
00:03:41,581 --> 00:03:45,296
a reddish brown. It's still called the Red Forest,

64
00:03:45,296 --> 00:03:49,012
and it remains one of the most contaminated places

65
00:03:49,012 --> 00:03:49,903
in the zone.

66
00:03:50,453 --> 00:03:53,587
Deep in the basement of Reactor 4 lies one

67
00:03:53,587 --> 00:03:57,094
of the most famous objects of the disaster: the

68
00:03:57,094 --> 00:04:01,272
Elephant's Foot, a mass of melted nuclear fuel, concrete

69
00:04:01,272 --> 00:04:05,003
and metal that flowed down like lava and hardened.

70
00:04:05,553 --> 00:04:08,420
When it was found in late 1986, it was

71
00:04:08,420 --> 00:04:12,193
so radioactive that standing next to it could give

72
00:04:12,193 --> 00:04:15,287
you a deadly dose in a matter of minutes.

73
00:04:15,287 --> 00:04:19,740
Workers photographed it using cameras on wheels and mirrors

74
00:04:19,740 --> 00:04:20,721
to stay safe.

75
00:04:21,271 --> 00:04:26,131
It's much less radioactive today, but still extremely dangerous.

76
00:04:26,131 --> 00:04:30,536
And strangely, scientists found black fungi growing on the

77
00:04:30,536 --> 00:04:35,244
walls inside the reactor, seemingly thriving in the radiation.

78
00:04:35,794 --> 00:04:39,648
Within months of the accident, workers built a concrete

79
00:04:39,648 --> 00:04:43,923
shelter, called the sarcophagus, over the ruined reactor. But

80
00:04:43,923 --> 00:04:46,446
it was built in a hurry and began to

81
00:04:46,446 --> 00:04:46,866
crack.

82
00:04:47,416 --> 00:04:51,950
Hundreds of thousands of people, known as liquidators, worked

83
00:04:51,950 --> 00:04:56,856
on the cleanup: soldiers, miners, engineers and firefighters. Some

84
00:04:56,856 --> 00:05:01,093
cleared radioactive debris from the reactor roof by hand,

85
00:05:01,093 --> 00:05:03,992
in shifts lasting only a minute or two.

86
00:05:04,542 --> 00:05:07,679
So in 2016, a giant steel arch called the

87
00:05:07,679 --> 00:05:11,275
New Safe Confinement was slid over it on rails.

88
00:05:11,275 --> 00:05:15,254
It's about one hundred eight meters tall, big enough

89
00:05:15,254 --> 00:05:18,850
to cover the Statue of Liberty, and designed to

90
00:05:18,850 --> 00:05:20,457
last a hundred years.

91
00:05:21,007 --> 00:05:25,397
It's the largest movable land structure ever built. Inside,

92
00:05:25,397 --> 00:05:28,894
robotic cranes are meant to help take apart the

93
00:05:28,894 --> 00:05:30,308
old reactor safely.

94
00:05:30,858 --> 00:05:35,182
In recent years, the war in Ukraine reached Chernobyl

95
00:05:35,182 --> 00:05:39,099
too. Russian troops occupied the site for over a

96
00:05:39,099 --> 00:05:42,689
month in 2022. And in February 2025, a drone

97
00:05:42,689 --> 00:05:46,606
struck the roof of the New Safe Confinement. The

98
00:05:46,606 --> 00:05:51,338
IAEA reported that radiation levels outside stayed normal.

99
00:05:51,888 --> 00:05:55,872
Now for the strangest part. With people gone, nature

100
00:05:55,872 --> 00:05:59,780
came back. Wolves, lynx, elk, boar, bears and bison

101
00:05:59,780 --> 00:06:03,381
roam the exclusion zone today, in some areas in

102
00:06:03,381 --> 00:06:06,523
greater numbers than before the accident.

103
00:06:07,073 --> 00:06:12,377
Rare Przewalski's horses, released here in the late 1990s,

104
00:06:12,377 --> 00:06:16,310
now live wild among the abandoned villages.

105
00:06:16,860 --> 00:06:20,196
And not everyone left. A small number of mostly

106
00:06:20,196 --> 00:06:25,448
elderly residents, called self-settlers, returned to their villages inside

107
00:06:25,448 --> 00:06:29,138
the zone after the accident, choosing to spend their

108
00:06:29,138 --> 00:06:31,409
lives at home despite the risks.

109
00:06:31,959 --> 00:06:36,197
Scientists still debate how healthy these animals are. But

110
00:06:36,197 --> 00:06:39,996
the lesson seems clear: for wildlife, the absence of

111
00:06:39,996 --> 00:06:43,138
humans can matter even more than radiation.

112
00:06:43,688 --> 00:06:46,923
Would you visit Chernobyl if tours opened again? Tell

113
00:06:46,923 --> 00:06:48,083
me in the comments.

114
00:06:48,633 --> 00:06:52,957
Before the war, Chernobyl became a tourist attraction. In

115
00:06:52,957 --> 00:06:57,130
2019, more than one hundred thousand people visited. On

116
00:06:57,130 --> 00:07:01,531
a typical day tour, following the rules, visitors received

117
00:07:01,531 --> 00:07:05,401
a radiation dose similar to a long airplane flight.

118
00:07:05,951 --> 00:07:09,695
The danger today is in the hotspots: certain soils,

119
00:07:09,695 --> 00:07:14,026
metal and objects that still hold radiation. Picking things

120
00:07:14,026 --> 00:07:18,136
up, eating anything grown there, or entering the reactor

121
00:07:18,136 --> 00:07:20,265
itself is strictly forbidden.

122
00:07:20,815 --> 00:07:25,653
Some radioactive elements will be around for a very

123
00:07:25,653 --> 00:07:31,249
long time. Cesium-137 loses half its radioactivity in about

124
00:07:31,249 --> 00:07:37,605
thirty years. Plutonium-239 takes about twenty-four thousand years.

125
00:07:38,155 --> 00:07:42,442
So, could you survive Chernobyl? Inside Reactor 4 in

126
00:07:42,442 --> 00:07:45,987
1986, no one could. In the zone today, with

127
00:07:45,987 --> 00:07:49,450
a guide and strict rules, a short visit is

128
00:07:49,450 --> 00:07:53,407
possible. But living there for good, the zone is

129
00:07:53,407 --> 00:07:57,035
expected to stay off-limits for generations.

130
00:07:57,585 --> 00:08:01,553
Last question. Which extreme place should we explore next?

131
00:08:01,553 --> 00:08:03,195
Tell me in the comments.

132
00:08:03,745 --> 00:08:07,437
Thanks for watching Earth Decoded. If you enjoyed this,

133
00:08:07,437 --> 00:08:10,525
like the video, subscribe, and share it with a

134
00:08:10,525 --> 00:08:13,546
friend who loves history. See you in the next

135
00:08:13,546 --> 00:08:13,815
one.

